{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Elohim tested Abraham.</b> This is the pericope of the Binding (akedah), by virtue of which Israel are assured that the merit of the Binding shields them in every generation. And testing — far be it — does not pertain to HaShem, blessed be He, but rather to creatures, in order to make known the greatness of the stature of the righteous man or the prophet. And thus Scripture says (Psalms 11:5): ״ה' צדיק יבחן״. And the fact that the Binding is juxtaposed to the pericope of Abimelech — in the Midrash they say that נסה is an expression of wrath, as we translate [in Aramaic]: זעפים — נסיסין. And this corresponds to that which angered the Holy One, blessed be He, on account of the seven ewes that he gave to Abimelech; measure for measure his descendants were punished by the Ark of the Covenant dwelling in the field of the Philistines for seven months." "<b>And know</b> that the test of the Binding was in order to proclaim among the nations the great obligation of fear and love toward the blessed Name. For love is of three levels. <b>The first:</b> one who loves the king, and out of his love speaks his praise and proclaims his virtues constantly all day, and makes known to all his mighty deeds — but would not part with any of his money for the sake of his love. <b>The second:</b> one who loves him more than the first — he speaks his praise and gives all his money for his sake, but would not surrender himself to death for his sake. <b>The third:</b> one who loves him more than both of them — he speaks his praise and gives all his money for his sake, and surrenders himself to death." "Now Abraham was complete in all three of these levels. <b>The first:</b> he spoke the praise of the King of kings, the Holy One, blessed be He, and proclaimed His divinity and His oneness in the world, as it is written: ״ויקרא שם בשם ה' אל עולם״ — for the people of his generation attributed the governance of the world to the stars and the constellations, and he would guide them in the way of truth and make known to them the sole Guide, who is El of the universe — as the saying of Eliphaz (Job 5:8): ״ואל אלהים אשים דברתי״ — he says: the governance of the world let him not assign to the forces of the spheres and the host of heaven, but only to the blessed Name. <b>The second:</b> he distributed, gave to the destitute for the love of HaShem, blessed be He. For he kept an inn, and his doors were open to every passerby. And thus they said in the Midrash: four doors did the house of Abraham have, facing the four winds of the world, and whoever entered by one door would exit by another, so that he would not be embarrassed before wayfarers. And thus it is intimated in the verse: ״ויטע אש״ל בבאר שבע״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted [the word אש״ל as]: אכילה, שכיבה, לויה — eating, lodging, escorting. And not only that, but he would run after them and race to meet them, as it says: ״וירץ לקראתם״. And of him Scripture said (Proverbs 11:24): ״יש מפזר ונוסף עוד״. And there is no doubt that he gave all the wealth of his household out of love for HaShem, blessed be He. <b>The third:</b> he surrendered himself to death for the love of HaShem when he was cast into the fiery furnace for the sanctification of HaShem's name, blessed be He. And because he attained the level of love and traversed all three levels, the blessed Name called him ״אברהם אוהבי״ — Abraham My beloved. But in this matter of the Binding, Abraham demonstrated the power of his love for the blessed Name, which was so strong and mighty that one cannot assess it alongside the three aforementioned levels, nor compare it to one who surrenders himself to death for the sake of a friend or for the sake of a master. For Abraham would have been worthy, had he a hundred bodies, to surrender them all to death on account of Isaac. But this deed was not like the other deeds, and this test was not like the other tests: nature cannot endure it, and the imaginative faculty cannot conceive it — that a man should be old and aged, a hundred years old, possessing wealth and property and honor, and who craved a son with the utmost craving, and after despair had a son — one must reckon the strength of the powerful love for that son, and the love would double and redouble by the end of a year beyond what it was when he was eight days old, and it would double at five and ten years beyond what it was when he was one year old, for by then he is already grown and distinguishes between good and evil; and the love would double again as he approached forty years — that is the time when a man's intellect reaches perfection. And this was Isaac, who was thirty-seven years old at the time of the Binding. And the love would increase yet more and more when he was commanded to slaughter this son — he himself, with his own hands. And this love — there is nothing in the world that could overcome it. And Abraham, because he was complete in faith, despite the intensity of his love for Isaac, recognized that it was fitting that the love of HaShem, blessed be He, should prevail over the love of Isaac. And by the force of the power of this surpassing love that Abraham had for the blessed Name, the love of Isaac became light in his eyes, and he set out to slaughter him after three days had passed since the utterance was spoken to him. For had he done so suddenly and immediately in his place, his deed would have been done in haste and alarm. But since it came after a three-day journey, it was already done with settled mind and deliberation. And this is what they said in Bereshit Rabbah: Why on the third day and not on the first day or the second? So that the nations of the world should not say: he was confused and bewildered, and went and slaughtered his son; therefore he performed that deed in full composure. And along with this he was tested in the quality of fear and in the quality of love — fear, not fear of punishment; and love, not love in anticipation of reward, that is, on condition of receiving a prize, for that would not be pure love, for there is one who drinks a bitter drink knowingly, because it will benefit him in the end. And this is what was said: ״עתה ידעתי כי ירא אלהים אתה״ — now I know that you are one who fears Elohim." "<b>And it is known</b> that the knowledge [belongs] to the blessed Name at all times. But the meaning of the matter: it is known from now, that it had passed from potentiality into actuality. And observe too the alacrity of Abraham and his diligence in this commandment — for he could have taken along a retinue of men from the people of his household, but he took with him only two; thus it is written: ״שני נעריו עמו״ — his two lads with him, Ishmael and Eliezer. And even those two lads were not with him at the time of the Binding, but he said to them: ״שבו לכם פה עם החמור״ — sit here with the donkey. And the reason was so that they would not prevent this deed from him, and so that they would not stay his hand."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:14:24.417221124Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}